<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macarius, Metropolitan of Moscow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1482–1563</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Macarius of Moscow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macarius was Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and All Rus' from 1542 to 1563 and a leading adviser of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>, whom he crowned as tsar in the Dormition Cathedral on 16 January 1547. Earlier archbishop of Novgorod, he compiled the Great Menaion Reader, a vast collection of religious texts, presided over councils in 1547 and 1549 that canonised many Russian saints, and led the Hundred Chapters Council of 1551.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macarius-metropolitan-of-moscow.jpg" alt="Macarius, Metropolitan of Moscow" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian icon painter (before 1917)Public domain image (according to PD-Russia-expired) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macarius_of_Moscow_(19-20th_c.,_Atheism_museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
